---
[v1] arm64: dts: mediatek: fix mt7988 watchdog interrupt number

The MT7988 TOPRGU watchdog interrupt is GIC_SPI 74, not GIC_SPI 110.
Fix the interrupt number so the watchdog pretimeout (bark) IRQ reaches
the mtk_wdt driver.

Background:
mtk_wdt_probe() requests the IRQ from DTS. On success it sets
pretimeout = timeout / 2, and mtk_wdt_start() enables
WDT_MODE_DUAL_EN | WDT_MODE_IRQ_EN. In dual mode, the 1st timeout
raises an IRQ instead of resetting the SoC. Only a 2nd timeout causes
a HW reset.

SPI 110 is a valid GIC line, so devm_request_irq() still succeeds with
the wrong number. The driver therefore enables dual mode, but the WDT
asserts SPI 74, which no driver has requested and the GIC never
enabled. The bark is lost, the 2nd timeout fires, and every watchdog
timeout looks like a full system hang (HW reset).

Flow after the 1st timeout (pretimeout):

  | Step | SPI 110 (wrong)           | SPI 74 (correct)                 |
  |------|---------------------------|----------------------------------|
  | 1    | WDT asserts SPI 74        | WDT asserts SPI 74               |
  | 2    | no handler, IRQ ignored   | mtk_wdt_isr()                    |
  | 3    | -                         | watchdog_notify_pretimeout()     |
  | 4    | -                         | pretimeout_panic() -> panic()    |
  | 5    | -                         | PANIC_TIMEOUT=1 -> emergency     |
  |      |                           | restart -> machine_restart()     |
  | 6    | -                         | psci_sys_reset() (prio 129)      |
  |      |                           | -> SMC PSCI SYSTEM_RESET         |
  | 7    | -                         | ATF mtk_plat_system_reset()      |
  |      |                           | -> mtk_wdt_soft_reset()          |
  |      |                           | -> write WDT_SWRST (0x1209)      |
  | 8    | 2nd timeout -> HW reset   | -                                |
  | Next | WDT_STA = 0x80000000      | WDT_STA = 0x40000000             |
  | boot | "Watchdog timeout"        | "Software reset (reboot)"        |
  | log  | no panic log/backtrace    | panic log + backtrace on console |

Reboot reason in WDT_STA (0x1001c00c), printed by BL2
mtk_wdt_print_status() as "WDT: [xxxxxxxx] <reason>":

  | Bit | Reason   | Triggered by                                      |
  |-----|----------|---------------------------------------------------|
  | 31  | HW reset | WDT counter expiry with no IRQ handling:          |
  |     |          | - dual mode 2nd timeout (bark lost / CPUs hung)   |
  |     |          | - dual + IRQ disabled, WDT enabled and not fed    |
  | 30  | SW reset | Write to WDT_SWRST:                               |
  |     |          | - reboot cmd / panic via PSCI -> ATF              |
  |     |          | - mtk_wdt_restart() (prio 128, PSCI is used 1st)  |
  |     |          | - pretimeout panic (needs correct IRQ, this fix)  |

WDT_MODE combinations (WDT_MODE_EN = 1, not fed):

  | DUAL_EN | IRQ_EN | IRQ route | 1st timeout  | 2nd timeout | Result   |
  |---------|--------|-----------|--------------|-------------|----------|
  | 0       | 0      | -         | reset        | -           | HW reset |
  | 1       | 1      | no (110)  | IRQ lost     | HW reset    | HW reset |
  | 1       | 1      | yes (74)  | IRQ -> panic | -           | SW reset |

After this fix a HW reset again means a real hard hang (IRQs dead on
all CPUs). A plain userspace/kernel stall (watchdog not fed, IRQs still
alive) now panics with a backtrace and ends with a SW reset.

---
v1: Fix mt7988 watchdog interrupt number from GIC_SPI 110 to GIC_SPI 74
---

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt7988a.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt7988a.dtsi
@@ -219,7 +219,7 @@
 		watchdog: watchdog@1001c000 {
 			compatible = "mediatek,mt7988-wdt";
 			reg = <0 0x1001c000 0 0x1000>;
-			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 110 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 74 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
 			#reset-cells = <1>;
 		};
 
